# The proxy in front of mldsa-authd (spec docs/mldsa-authd-spec.md §7.1, §7.2).
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONE UNDER TEST. tests/caddy_proxy.sh copies it into a
# container, runs `caddy validate` on it, starts a real Caddy with it, and
# performs a real post-quantum login through it -- so an example that drifted
# from what works would fail the leg rather than mislead an operator.
#
# Two things matter and both are load-bearing:
#
#   proxy_protocol v2
#       The preamble Caddy prepends before the client is allowed to write a
#       byte. It is the ONLY unforgeable statement of the client's address the
#       daemon can get, because everything else it can see is Caddy. Without
#       it the daemon has no address, refuses the connection (Req 12), and
#       says `event=closed-protocol` in the journal.
#
#   a Unix-socket upstream
#       The daemon's proxy-facing listener is a Unix socket, mode 0660, whose
#       peer uid must appear in the daemon's `proxy_uids` (Req 11). The
#       preamble is only trustworthy because the peer that wrote it is the
#       proxy; run Caddy as a user that is in that list and nothing else.
#
# WebSocket needs no directive: reverse_proxy passes an Upgrade through.
#
# Replace the site address and let Caddy manage the certificate; the TLS half
# is Caddy's job and the daemon never sees it.

authd.example.com {
	reverse_proxy /authd/v1* unix//run/mldsa-authd/p.sock {
		transport http {
			proxy_protocol v2
		}
	}
}
